  <section id="async">
  
  <h2>21. Async: Task vs Promise — in depth</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>async Task&lt;string&gt; FetchAsync(string url, CancellationToken ct)
&#123;
  using var http = new HttpClient();
  return await http.GetStringAsync(url, ct);
&#125;
string body = await FetchAsync(url, ct);

// parallel (both start immediately)
Task&lt;string&gt; a = FetchAsync(u1, ct);
Task&lt;string&gt; b = FetchAsync(u2, ct);
string[] both = await Task.WhenAll(a, b);

// fire-and-forget with safety:
_ = Task.Run(() =&gt; Background(ct));</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>async function fetchText(url: string, signal?: AbortSignal): Promise&lt;string&gt; &#123;
  const res = await fetch(url, &#123; signal &#125;);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP $&#123;res.status&#125;`);
  return res.text();
&#125;
const body = await fetchText(url, signal);

// parallel
const [a, b] = await Promise.all([fetchText(u1, signal), fetchText(u2, signal)]);
// tolerant: Promise.allSettled([...])
// first-wins: Promise.race / Promise.any</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>C#</th><th>TS</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Task&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>Promise&lt;T&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Task.WhenAll</code></td><td><code>Promise.all</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Task.WhenAny</code></td><td><code>Promise.race</code> / <code>Promise.any</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>CancellationToken</code></td><td><code>AbortSignal</code> / <code>AbortController</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>IAsyncEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> + <code>await foreach</code></td><td><code>AsyncGenerator</code> + <code>for await...of</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Parallel.ForEachAsync</code></td><td><code>p-limit</code> + <code>Promise.all</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>Concurrency limits + cancellation</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>// bounded parallelism built-in
await Parallel.ForEachAsync(urls, ct, async (u, t) =&gt;
&#123; await FetchAsync(u, t); &#125;);

// cooperative cancellation
ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>import pLimit from "p-limit";
const limit = pLimit(5);
await Promise.all(urls.map((u) =&gt; limit(() =&gt; fetchText(u, signal))));

const ctrl = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() =&gt; ctrl.abort(), 5000);
await fetchText(url, ctrl.signal); // throws AbortError</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <h3>Async streams</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>await foreach (var item in StreamAsync(ct)) Console.WriteLine(item);

async IAsyncEnumerable&lt;int&gt; StreamAsync(
  [EnumeratorCancellation] CancellationToken ct)
&#123; for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) yield return i; &#125;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>for await (const item of stream()) console.log(item);

async function* stream(): AsyncGenerator&lt;number&gt; &#123;
  for (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) yield i;
&#125;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>Both use <code>async/await</code>, but C# continuations run on the thread pool; TS runs on the event-loop microtask queue.</li>
    <li>Don’t block: C# <code>.Result/.Wait()</code> can deadlock; TS <code>await</code> never blocks the thread.</li>
    <li>Threads vs event loop: CPU-bound work needs threads in both — C# has them by default, TS needs <code>Worker</code> threads or offload.</li>
  </ul>

